The table structure information is (table names): 
['countries', 'customers', 'promotions', 'products', 'times', 'channels', 'sales', 'costs', 'supplementary_demographics', 'currency']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns, their possible usage and the main filters we will need
• countries.country_name : to find the country we need (“Italy”).  
• countries.country_id   : joins to customers.country_id.  
• customers.cust_id      : the customer key that finally gets bucketed.  
• sales.cust_id          : joins to customers.cust_id so that we only keep the Italian buyers.  
• sales.time_id          : filter to the month we want ( 2021‑12‑01 … 2021‑12‑31 ).  
• sales.prod_id / promo_id / channel_id : join keys to costs in order to read unit prices / costs that correspond to the same transaction.  
• sales.quantity_sold    : used in the profit formula.  
• costs.unit_price – costs.unit_cost : margin per unit; together with quantity_sold gives the profit for one sales row.  
• costs.time_id / promo_id / channel_id / prod_id : used to line‑up the correct price/cost record with each sales row.

Main conditions  
1. countries.country_name = 'Italy'                                 (Italian customers)  
2. sales.time_id BETWEEN '2021‑12‑01' AND '2021‑12‑31'               (December 2021)  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Ten exploratory → final SQL statements
--------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1) Check that we have the correct country_id for Italy
SELECT DISTINCT "country_id", "country_name"
FROM "countries"
WHERE "country_name" = 'Italy'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List the first Italian customers (cust_id & name parts)
SELECT DISTINCT c."cust_id",
       c."cust_first_name" || ' ' || c."cust_last_name" AS "cust_name"
FROM   "customers"  AS c
JOIN   "countries"  AS co
       ON c."country_id" = co."country_id"
WHERE  co."country_name" = 'Italy'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
